WHAT COULD HAPPEN TODAY
→ If your Mac is stolen, the thief could read your files, saved passwords and
  photos without your password, by taking out the disk or starting the Mac
  from another drive.

FIX FIRST (essential for you) (1)

✗ Turn on disk encryption (FileVault)
  Now: FileVault is Off.
  Why it matters:
    Without FileVault, anyone who steals your Mac can take the disk out or
    boot it from another drive and read all your files, passwords and photos
    without knowing your password. With FileVault on, the data is useless
    without it.
  How to fix it:
    1. Open System Settings > Privacy & Security.
    2. Scroll to FileVault and click "Turn On".
    3. Save the recovery key somewhere safe outside the Mac (or let your Apple
       Account unlock it).
    4. Keep working while it encrypts; it can take a few hours.
  Open: open "x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.settings.PrivacySecurity.extension"

RECOMMENDED (1)

✗ Turn on the firewall's stealth mode
  Now: Firewall stealth mode is off
  Why it matters:
    In stealth mode your Mac does not answer "is anyone there?" probes, so it
    is harder to find on a public network. It only works while the firewall is
    on.
  How to fix it:
    1. Open System Settings > Network > Firewall > Options.
    2. Turn on "Enable stealth mode".
  Open: open "x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Network-Settings.extension"
  Or in Terminal: sudo /usr/libexec/ApplicationFirewall/socketfilterfw --setstealthmode on

SUGGESTIONS (optional for you) (1)

· Consider a standard account for everyday use
  Now: staff everyone admin
  Why it matters:
    Your everyday account is an administrator. Anything you run (including a
    malicious app you are tricked into opening) can ask for admin rights with
    the same password you type all day. A separate admin account adds one more
    barrier. This is a suggestion, not an urgent problem.
  How to fix it:
    1. Open System Settings > Users & Groups and add a new Administrator
       account with a different password.
    2. Log in to that new account once to check it works.
    3. Go back to Users & Groups, open your usual account and turn off "Allow
       this user to administer this computer".
    4. From now on, macOS will ask for the admin account's name and password
       when it needs to.
  Open: open "x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Users-Groups-Settings.extension"

CHECK IT YOURSELF (1)

? Make sure Find My Mac is on
  Why it matters:
    Find My lets you see where your Mac is, lock it with a message and erase
    it remotely if it is stolen. It also enables Activation Lock, which makes
    a stolen Mac much harder to resell.
  How to fix it:
    1. Open System Settings and click your name at the top.
    2. Go to iCloud > Find My Mac (or "See All" > Find My Mac) and turn it on.
    3. Test it once at icloud.com/find from another device.

